     "type": "intro",
     "title": "A Lawyer Tries to Map Everything",
     "content": "Picture London in 1605. A 44-year-old lawyer named **Francis Bacon** — clever, ambitious, short of money, and repeatedly passed over for the government jobs he craved — publishes a book in English with an outrageous goal: to survey *everything humans know*, mark what is missing, and tell the new king where to invest.\n\nThe book is **The Advancement of Learning**, two books long and dedicated to **King James I**. It is part flattery, part manifesto, part blueprint. Out of it, and out of the larger project it launched, grew the idea that knowledge should be built carefully from evidence and put to work for human good. The Royal Society, founded in 1660, would later honour Bacon as its intellectual patron.\n\nOver these 12 lessons you will walk through Bacon's map one region at a time. And you will carry one sentence with you the whole way — a sentence about how to start thinking if you want to end up somewhere solid: **begin with doubts.**",
