     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Born Into Power, Bored by Aristotle",
     "content": "Bacon was born on **22 January 1561** at York House on the Strand in London. His father, **Sir Nicholas Bacon**, was Elizabeth I's Lord Keeper of the Great Seal, one of the most powerful men in England. His mother, **Lady Anne Bacon** (born Cooke), was one of the most learned women of her age: fluent in Latin and Greek, she translated Bishop Jewel's Latin defence of the Church of England into English.\n\nAt just **12**, Francis went up to **Trinity College, Cambridge** (1573) with his older brother Anthony. There, as his chaplain William Rawley later reported, he first came to dislike the philosophy of **Aristotle** — not because Aristotle was a fool, but because the method was strong for disputing and winning arguments and barren at producing *works* that benefit human life. That complaint is the seed of everything you will read in this book.\n\n• **1576** — sent to Paris with the ambassador Sir Amyas Paulet to train for public service\n• **1579** — his father dies suddenly, leaving Francis, a younger son, with little money\n• **1579 onward** — settles at **Gray's Inn** to study law; called to the bar in 1582\n• **1584** — enters the House of Commons\n\nSo the man who set out to reform all knowledge was trained not as a scientist but as a **lawyer**, someone whose daily work was weighing evidence and testimony.",
