     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Essex, Ambition, and Betrayal",
     "content": "For twenty years Bacon chased high office and kept missing. His great patron was the dashing **Robert Devereux, Earl of Essex**, Elizabeth's favourite. In 1594 Essex pushed hard to make Bacon **Attorney-General**; the Queen gave the post to **Edward Coke** instead — Bacon's lifelong rival, who soon afterwards also married the rich widow Bacon had hoped to wed. A bid to become **Solicitor-General** failed in 1595 too. Essex consoled him with a gift of land at Twickenham.\n\nIn **1597** Bacon published his first little book of ten **Essays**, bound with Latin religious meditations, the *Meditationes Sacrae*. (That is where the Latin phrase usually rendered \"knowledge is power\" appears — not in the Advancement.)\n\nThen it went dark. In **February 1601** Essex led a failed rising in the streets of London. Bacon, who had already distanced himself after giving advice Essex ignored, helped **prosecute** his old friend, and afterwards was employed to write the government's official account justifying the verdict. Essex was beheaded on **25 February 1601**.\n\nKeep this in mind as you read: the author of a book about pure, useful truth was also a hungry courtier who knew exactly how power works. Both sides of Bacon show up on the page.",
