     "explanation": "Bacon says the work is not meant for curiosity or the satisfaction of book-lovers, but \"for a more serious and grave purpose\": to make learned men wise \"in the use and administration of learning,\" as church history makes a divine wiser than reading theologians alone. He does praise his own age, especially its voyages, but the history of learning was not a victory lap. And history answers to memory; it complements philosophy rather than replacing it.",
