     "type": "concept",
     "title": "The Missing Books of Business",
     "content": "Here Bacon flags one of his biggest **desiderata**. Of the three civil wisdoms, behaviour \"hath been elegantly handled,\" and government has a literature. But \"for the wisdom of business, wherein man's life is most conversant, there be no books of it, except some few scattered advertisements.\" That gap, he argues, is why people believe learning and practical wisdom don't mix.\n\nHe insists it *can* be taught. Roman senators like Coruncanius and Laelius used to walk the Forum at set hours so citizens could consult them about a daughter's marriage, a son's career, a purchase or a lawsuit. Quintus Cicero wrote his brother a campaign handbook, *De petitione consulatus* — \"the only book of business that I know written by the ancients.\" And Solomon's Proverbs, which Bacon mines for pages, are practical counsel.\n\nBest of all, he says, is the form Machiavelli used: **discourse upon histories or examples**, because \"knowledge drawn freshly and in our view out of particulars, knoweth the way best to particulars again.\" Lives suit private business; wise letters, like Cicero's to Atticus, suit it even better.\n\nWhat about **government**? Writing to a king who was \"a master of this science,\" Bacon tactfully passes over it \"in silence\" — noting only that laws had been written by philosophers and lawyers, but never by statesmen.",
